Why Running as Root is Dangerous

By default, container engines like Docker execute container processes as the root user (UID 0). If an attacker successfully exploits a vulnerability and breaks out of the container, 
they inherit root privileges on the host operating system, potentially giving them complete control over your entire infrastructure. 

1. Implement Non-Root Execution

Enforcing the principle of least privilege is the most vital step in container security. 
• In the Dockerfile: Create a dedicated application user and switch to them before running the entry point. 

dockerfile
FROM alpine:latest
# Create a system user and group
RUN addgroup -S appgroup && adduser -S appuser -G appgroup

# Set appropriate ownership for application directories
WORKDIR /app
COPY --chown=appuser:appgroup . .

# Switch to the non-root user
USER appuser
CMD ["./my-application"]

Use code with caution.
• At Container Runtime: If you cannot modify the container image, force a user change when starting the container:
	• Docker: Use docker run --user 1001 my-image.
	• Kubernetes: Define the user within the securityContext block:

yaml
spec:
  securityContext:
    runAsNonRoot: true
    runAsUser: 1000
Use code with caution.

2. Core Container Security Best Practices

Beyond executing as a non-root user, apply these essential strategies to harden your containerized environments:

Best Practice - Implementation Strategy - 	Impact
1. Use Minimal Base -  Images	Build with Distroless or alpine-based images; leverage multi-stage builds.	 - Minimizes the attack surface by stripping out unnecessary tools like package managers, curl, or shells.
2. Make the Filesystem Read-Only  - 	Pass --read-only in Docker or set readOnlyRootFilesystem: true in Kubernetes.	 
- Prevents attackers from downloading malicious binaries, scripts, or writing persistent backdoors.
3. Drop Linux Capabilities	 -  Strip default system permissions by applying --cap-drop=ALL, then only add back what is explicitly required.	 
- Restricts access to sensitive kernel interactions, reducing vulnerability to kernel-level privilege escalations.
4. Externalize Secrets	 -  Never hardcode environment variables, tokens, or API keys into Dockerfiles.  
-  Inject them at runtime using systems like HashiCorp Vault or native Kubernetes Secrets.	Stops API keys and sensitive operational data from leaking through public or exposed image registries.
5. Enable Automated Vulnerability Scanning	 - Embed automated tools like Trivy or Docker Scout directly into your CI/CD pipelines.	
 -  Flags known software vulnerabilities (CVEs) and insecure defaults before code reaches your production environment.
6. Deploy Rootless Runtimes	 -  Configure your underlying engine to run in Docker Rootless Mode or switch to an alternate engine like Podman.	
 - Ensures the entire container engine daemon runs as an unprivileged host user, providing isolation from the system kernel.

